You are given an integer array prices where prices[i] is the price of a given stock on the ith day, and an integer k.
Find the maximum profit you can achieve. You may complete at most k transactions: i.e. you may buy at most k times and
sell at most k times. Note: You may not engage in multiple transactions simultaneously (i.e., you must sell the stock
before you buy again).

Example 1:
Input: k = 2, prices = [2,4,1]
Output: 2
Explanation: Buy on day 1 (price = 2) and sell on day 2 (price = 4), profit = 4-2 = 2.

Example 2:
Input: k = 2, prices = [3,2,6,5,0,3]
Output: 7
Explanation: Buy on day 2 (price = 2) and sell on day 3 (price = 6), profit = 6-2 = 4. Then buy on day 5 (price = 0) and
sell on day 6 (price = 3), profit = 3-0 = 3.

Constraints:
1 <= k <= 100
1 <= prices.length <= 1000
0 <= prices[i] <= 1000

Approach:
This problem is similar to the 'Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Transaction Fee'. The difference lies in the extra
parameter that gets added to this problem that is k(i.e. transactions). Due to this there gets added an extra dimension
to the dp array as each day now also differs with every value of k. The tabulation space optimised solution also has a
subtle java optimisation in the code where we swap the ahead and the curr arrays using a temp array variable this does
not effect the overall complexity but makes code run faster by reducing the java garbage collection activity.

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space) Note: k does not effect the levels/depth of the
recursion stack at any given point of time. So each call makes up to 2 recursive calls, and every recursive call can go
n levels deep.
Memoization -> TC = O(n*k), SC = O(n*k) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n*k)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n*k), SC = O(n*k)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation -> TC = O(n*k), SC = O(k)
